WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:06.379
2021 was a banner year for science fiction
movies, filled to the brim with Kaiju showdowns,

00:00:06.379 --> 00:00:11.140
time-loop extravaganzas, and classic franchise
fare. Here's the question, though: just how

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:15.490
many of these excellent sci-fi movies have
you seen?

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:20.070
Little Fish is a movie that blends romance
and drama with the world of science fiction.

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:24.520
Released in theaters and on streaming services
in February 2021, director Chad Hartigan's

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:29.440
movie is set in Seattle in the near-future,
in the midst of an epidemic of an Alzheimer's-like

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:34.909
illness known as NIA. Olivia Cooke and Jack
O'Connell star as Emma and Jude, a couple

00:00:34.909 --> 00:00:39.319
struggling with the effect of NIA on their
relationship and society. Little Fish was

00:00:39.319 --> 00:00:45.629
filmed before COVID-19 and was due to be shown
at the canceled 2020 Tribeca Film Festival.

00:00:45.629 --> 00:00:50.460
This means that its underlying premise is
coincidental — but incredibly timely, too.

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:54.269
Entertainment Weekly's Mary Sollosi writes
that, despite the subject matter, Little Fish

00:00:54.269 --> 00:00:58.979
resonates mostly as a love story. She also
praises the performances from Cooke and O'Connell,

00:00:58.979 --> 00:00:59.979
writing:

00:00:59.979 --> 00:01:04.339
"[They] both bring the cool project a necessary
warmth that sells their relationship, which

00:01:04.339 --> 00:01:05.860
is the real star here."

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:10.530
A number of critics have noted the film's
resemblance to 2004's Eternal Sunshine of

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:15.310
the Spotless Mind, though this movie still
has its own unique charms. Sollosi says the

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:20.070
sci-fi trapping gives the film's "central
question a poetic shape," while Jordan Raup

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:21.810
of The Film Stage notes that:

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:26.060
"The details of emotional bonds are given
utmost priority in the story over extraneous

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:29.159
exposition related to light sci-fi touches."

00:01:29.159 --> 00:01:33.469
In other words, if you're looking for a love
story fit for these trying times, Little Fish

00:01:33.469 --> 00:01:34.689
might be the one for you.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.000
Take a little romance, a little sci-fi, and
the basic premise of Groundhog Day, and you've

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:46.079
got The Map of Tiny Perfect Things. The plot
finds Kyle Allen as Mark, a smart teenager

00:01:46.079 --> 00:01:51.140
stuck in a time loop, who meets Kathryn Newton's
Margaret, another teenager stuck in the same

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:55.999
loop. The two subsequently decide to focus
on finding the tiny, perfect things of their

00:01:55.999 --> 00:01:57.960
endlessly repeating day.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.840
Released on Amazon Prime Video in February
2021, The Map of Tiny Perfect Things gathered

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:08.380
good notices for its strong cast and its emotional
resonance. Mike Ward at Should I See It writes:

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:13.380
"This is a detail-driven, meticulously crafted
film that stands apart from its peers with

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:19.130
two strong lead performances, a bit of a mischievous
grin, and a heart firmly entrenched on its

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:20.130
sleeve."

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:22.250
Meanwhile, Shani Harris of Culturess says:

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:27.420
"The Map of Tiny Perfect Things is a new spin
on young love that will stimulate your mind

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:28.420
and touch your heart."

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:33.920
If you're looking for some superheroes with
your sci-fi, then Zack Snyder's Justice League

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:40.100
is here to save the day. This epic HBO Max
adventure, which clocks in at a mere 4 hours

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:44.790
and 2 minutes, may have been met with a mixed
reaction from the critics, but DC's fans couldn't

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:46.760
possibly be happier.

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:51.760
Plot-wise, the film is broadly similar to
the 2017 movie Justice League. Superman's

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:56.070
sacrifice in Dawn of Justice has spurred Bats
to unite a team of his superfriends to face

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:57.750
off against a major cosmic threat.

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:02.400
"I don't care how many demons he's fought
in how many hells. He's never fought us. Not

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.720
us united."

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:10.290
The movie has six chapters and an epilogue
for easier viewing, and characters such as

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:15.720
Cyborg and the Flash have their stories significantly
fleshed out, giving the film a grander scale

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:18.500
with deeper meaning. The Age's Jake Wilson
writes:

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:23.250
"This is genuinely a different film from the
earlier Justice League, and a much more coherent

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:24.250
one."

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:28.730
A running theme in the critical response to
the so-called Snyder Cut is that Zack Snyder's

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:33.680
strongest and weakest tendencies are both
on display. Despite agreeing that the movie

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:36.980
"earns its self-importance," Vulture's Bilge
Ebiri notes:

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:41.090
"When you get the best of Snyder, you also
get the worst."

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:43.950
Meanwhile, Garry McConnachie of Glasgow Live
says:

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:48.020
"It's big, it's brash, and it's bombastic."

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:53.210
So if you love DC comics and you love Zack
Snyder, you're sure as heck gonna love this.

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:59.170
Launching on Hulu back in March 2021, Boss
Level features a hyper-violent time loop,

00:03:59.170 --> 00:04:04.531
a ton of action, and Frank Grillo playing
a badass former special forces agent. What

00:04:04.531 --> 00:04:06.590
more could you possibly want?

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:11.780
In this case, Roy Pulver's loop involves reliving
the day on which he meets a grisly death,

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:16.090
forcing him to figure out who killed him — and
why — so he can end the loop and rescue

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:21.320
his family. In order to do so, he has to find
Mel Gibson's Colonel Ventor and avoid a whole

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:26.110
army of killers along the way. Grillo in particular
drew a lot of praise for his leading role

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:31.060
in Boss Level. Richard Roeper of the Chicago
Sun-Times compliments Grillo's ability to

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:32.940
carry the film, saying that:

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:37.210
"He pulls it off with a combination of brute
force and light charm."

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:41.800
Indiewire's Christian Blauvelt also gives
Grillo high marks, saying the actor gives

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:44.260
the film "a pulse." He continues:

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:49.160
"He gets you to invest in Roy enough that,
even without a controller in your hands, you

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:52.550
never feel like you're simply watching someone
else play a video game."

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:58.700
Known as Seungriho in its original Korean,
Space Sweepers takes place in a dystopian

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:04.210
2092. The movie follows a crew of space junk
collectors who find a robot girl named Dorothy,

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:10.650
who is not only wanted by the evil corporation
UTS but can also be used as a weapon. Realizing

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:15.280
they can make a heap of money from their discovery,
the sweepers hold her for ransom — which

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:17.840
turns out to be a pretty bad idea.

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:23.540
Space Sweepers stars Kim Tae-ri as Captain
Jang and Richard Armitage as UTS' Elon Musk-like

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:29.730
gazillionaire, Sullivan. South Korean writer/director
Jo Sung-Hee is the man behind the camera,

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:34.990
previously known for his 2012 hit A Werewolf
Boy and 2016's Phantom Detective.

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:41.050
Netflix released Space Sweepers in February
2021, to mostly positive reviews. Daniel Hart

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:42.680
at Ready Steady Cut writes:

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:48.150
"Space Sweepers is an energetic space adventure
that manages to slightly emotionally engage

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:52.320
the audience while stimulating them with fun
action sequences."

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:54.680
While The Stranger's Charles Mudede says:

00:05:54.680 --> 00:06:00.510
"[Space Sweepers] features lots of explosions,
lots of robots, and that raw examination of

00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:05.139
capitalist class structures we have come to
expect from the best of South Korea's directors."

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:12.200
Supposedly the last installment of Legendary's
MonsterVerse, Godzilla vs. Kong is directed

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:17.860
by Adam Wingard and features a host of franchise
newcomers alongside returning faces such as

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:20.710
Millie Bobbie Brown and Kyle Chandler.

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:25.139
In Godzilla vs. Kong, the gargantuan Titans
have been dormant in the three years since

00:06:25.139 --> 00:06:30.450
Godzilla defeated King Ghidorah. Meanwhile,
the Hollow Earth theory discussed in Godzilla:

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:35.040
King of the Monsters has gained traction,
and the scientific agency known as Monarch

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:41.510
has been keeping tabs on Kong on Skull Island.
However, a mysterious force agitates Godzilla

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:46.980
into attacking the tech organization known
as Apex Cybernetics, and it's not long before

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:51.930
Kong has been co-opted into searching for
the Hollow Earth's power source. These threads

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:57.490
eventually converge when Godzilla's Kong-sense
kicks in, reigniting their ancient rivalry.

00:06:57.490 --> 00:07:03.169
Upon the movie's release in March 2021, critics
praised the movie for its kaiju action, with

00:07:03.169 --> 00:07:04.820
critic Peter Travers saying:

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:09.480
"The clash of these titans is spectacular
in every sense of the word."

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:14.020
Possibly the most glowing praise, however,
comes from Matt Zoller Seitz, who writes that:

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:19.490
"Godzilla vs. Kong just might be the best
studio film so far this year. If it isn't,

00:07:19.490 --> 00:07:21.570
it's for damn sure the most fun."

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:23.310
As a great man once said:

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:24.710
"Let them fight."

00:07:25.710 --> 00:07:31.190
An existentially fraught sci-fi drama released
on Netflix, Stowaway sends three astronauts

00:07:31.190 --> 00:07:35.950
to Mars on a two-year mission and then throws
a major wrench into their plans when — you

00:07:35.950 --> 00:07:39.370
guessed it — a stowaway is discovered on-board.

00:07:39.370 --> 00:07:43.480
Director Joe Penna assembled a great cast
for this movie, which stars Toni Collette

00:07:43.480 --> 00:07:48.800
as Marina Barnett, the ship's leader on her
final journey into space. Anna Kendrick plays

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:53.990
Zoe Levenson, the medical researcher, while
Daniel Dae Kim plays the ship's biologist,

00:07:53.990 --> 00:08:00.870
David Kim. Unbeknownst to them, Shamier Anderson's
Michael Adams is also aboard, albeit unwittingly

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:06.320
and somehow injured. Unfortunately, whatever
caused Adams' predicament has damaged the

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:11.150
ship's life-support controls, leaving enough
oxygen for only three people. In his review

00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:14.510
of Stowaway, Variety's Owen Gleiberman says:

00:08:14.510 --> 00:08:19.840
"Penna takes a mission to Mars and unfurls
it on a direct and intimate emotional level.

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:22.690
We always feel the characters could be us."

00:08:22.690 --> 00:08:26.290
Meanwhile, Christy Lemire of RogerEbert.com
writes:

00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:30.960
"[The movie is] a clever take on a familiar
genre with a terrific cast."

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:35.099
If you're in the mood for a classic sci-fi
flick with a dark twist, Stowaway could be

00:08:35.099 --> 00:08:36.399
the movie for you.

00:08:37.399 --> 00:08:42.289
The latest movie from director Alexandre Aja,
Oxygen has been hailed as a taut, engaging

00:08:42.289 --> 00:08:48.329
sci-fi thriller. In this Netflix French-language
film, a woman named Liz wakes up in a cryogenic

00:08:48.329 --> 00:08:54.029
pod with amnesia, not knowing who or where
she is. Her only companion is M.I.L.O., an

00:08:54.029 --> 00:08:58.619
artificial intelligence program, who advises
her that the oxygen levels of the pod are

00:08:58.619 --> 00:09:04.430
decreasing at an alarming rate. If Liz can't
figure out who she is and why she's there,

00:09:04.430 --> 00:09:05.959
she'll suffocate.

00:09:05.959 --> 00:09:12.279
Best known for the gory horror of 2003's slasher
High Tension and 2019's alligator thriller

00:09:12.279 --> 00:09:18.750
Crawl, Aja has been praised for his restraint
with this particular sci-fi gem. Filmed during

00:09:18.750 --> 00:09:24.170
COVID lockdown from a 2016 script by Christie
LeBlanc, Oxygen eschews the usual viscera

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:29.540
for a more existential sense of dread. As
David Rooney of The Hollywood Reporter writes:

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:34.240
"While cult followers of Aja's extreme horror
might be let down by his turn toward more

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:39.259
measured sci-fi, there's plenty of creepy
unease packed into his execution of a simple

00:09:39.259 --> 00:09:41.980
scenario that grows increasingly complicated."

00:09:41.980 --> 00:09:47.069
Meanwhile, Brian Tallerico of RogerEbert.com
notes that:

00:09:47.069 --> 00:09:51.540
"This truly feels like the most 'pandemic
thriller' yet in its own unpredictable way.

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:55.559
It's a film about isolation, loss, and an
uncertain future."

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:03.230
The long-awaited sequel to 2018's A Quiet
Place, A Quiet Place Part II had its theatrical-only

00:10:03.230 --> 00:10:08.660
release in May 2021, quickly earning praise
from critics and audiences alike.

00:10:08.660 --> 00:10:13.110
Director and screenwriter John Krasinski's
continuation of the Abbott family's story

00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:18.279
also provides a glimpse of the first day that
the sonic aliens arrived, allowing the beloved

00:10:18.279 --> 00:10:24.079
actor to briefly reprise his role as Lee Abbott.
Emily Blunt also returns as Evelyn Abbott,

00:10:24.079 --> 00:10:28.149
who journeys with her newborn infant and two
older children in search of a new place to

00:10:28.149 --> 00:10:33.519
live after their farm becomes uninhabitable.
They eventually come across Cillian Murphy's

00:10:33.519 --> 00:10:38.540
Emmett, a once-friendly acquaintance with
links to their past. Emmett, however, is now

00:10:38.540 --> 00:10:43.300
withdrawn and sullen, and Abbott no longer
knows if she can trust him. A Quiet Place

00:10:43.300 --> 00:10:48.790
Part II received praise for its tight script
and superior use of tension. Matthew Rozsa

00:10:48.790 --> 00:10:50.209
of Salon writes:

00:10:50.209 --> 00:10:54.629
"The film is packed with visceral moments
that stick to your ribs."

00:10:54.629 --> 00:10:57.540
Cary Darling of the Houston Chronicle also
points out that:

00:10:57.540 --> 00:11:02.119
"[The film is] a crafty exercise in tension,
editing, and sound design that [...] never

00:11:02.119 --> 00:11:04.089
wears out its welcome."

00:11:04.089 --> 00:11:09.440
Bloody Disgusting's Meagan Navarro has lavished
praise on deaf actress Millicent Simmonds

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:11.300
in particular, saying that:

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:14.939
"This is above all Simmonds' show, and she
nails it."

00:11:14.939 --> 00:11:19.019
So if you were worried about whether or not
this sequel would live up to the first film,

00:11:19.019 --> 00:11:22.449
rest assured — it's sure to be a terrifying
treat for horror fans.

00:11:23.449 --> 00:11:28.149
The Netflix sci-fi flick The Mitchells vs.
the Machines is a kid-friendly adventure that

00:11:28.149 --> 00:11:33.899
hit the streaming site in April 2021. This
comedy animation follows the Mitchells, a

00:11:33.899 --> 00:11:38.589
dysfunctional family who embark on a hectic
road trip. Unfortunately, their vacation turns

00:11:38.589 --> 00:11:44.139
into an apocalyptic battle for survival after
the beginning of a robotic revolution. The

00:11:44.139 --> 00:11:49.080
emphasis on technology-as-villain grounds
the film squarely in sci-fi territory as the

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:53.769
Mitchells, who've set aside their gadgets
for an old-fashioned vacation, are thrust

00:11:53.769 --> 00:11:54.929
into a digital war.

00:11:59.079 --> 00:12:03.429
The Mitchells vs. the Machines has been quite
the hit with critics, which might come as

00:12:03.429 --> 00:12:07.620
little surprise, considering it's produced
by Phil Lord and Christopher Miller, who are

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:14.019
otherwise known for Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse,
The Lego Movie, and 21 Jump Street. Bob Strauss

00:12:14.019 --> 00:12:18.720
of The San Francisco Chronicle calls the movie
"a machine apocalypse for the whole family,"

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:24.269
while other critics have noted the film's
surprising pro-tech stance. How's that? Well,

00:12:24.269 --> 00:12:26.279
The Guardian's Benjamin Lee writes:

00:12:26.279 --> 00:12:31.079
"Rather than the easier and clumsier film
[director Mike Rianda could have made, scolding

00:12:31.079 --> 00:12:35.769
kids for swiping and tapping and missing out
on real life, he shows that young digital

00:12:35.769 --> 00:12:40.699
creators are achieving incredible things and
that, when used right, this form of connectedness

00:12:40.699 --> 00:12:42.839
can be a powerful force."

00:12:43.839 --> 00:12:48.860
Free Guy stars Ryan Reynolds as the titular
Guy, a man who greets his goldfish, gets coffee,

00:12:48.860 --> 00:12:53.429
and goes to his job at the bank. Once he's
there, he talks with his best friend, security

00:12:53.429 --> 00:12:57.709
guard Buddy. And this is exactly what he does
every single day.

00:12:57.709 --> 00:13:02.079
As it turns out, Guy has no idea he's stuck
in a predetermined loop as an Non-Playing

00:13:02.079 --> 00:13:07.689
Character in a popular video game — that
is, until he meets Millie and becomes enamored.

00:13:07.689 --> 00:13:12.560
Millie wants to prove video game impresario
Antwan stole the game she developed, so she's

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:17.629
looking for proof before Antwan can destroy
the game… and Guy along with it.

00:13:17.629 --> 00:13:22.050
Directed by Shawn Levy, Free Guy received
a number of positive reviews from critics.

00:13:22.050 --> 00:13:26.319
Charlotte O'Sullivan of The Evening Standard
praises the humor in the movie, writing:

00:13:26.319 --> 00:13:31.329
"The humor in Free Guy is knowing rather than
nasty and, even at its most surreal, avoids

00:13:31.329 --> 00:13:32.879
coming across as indulgent."

00:13:32.879 --> 00:13:35.999
While Robert Daniels of The Playlist simply
writes:

00:13:35.999 --> 00:13:37.489
"Free Guy is a winner."

00:13:39.009 --> 00:13:44.649
Filmed on an iPhone during the COVID-19 lockdown,
the themes of the British film Infinitum:

00:13:44.649 --> 00:13:50.509
Subject Unknown are both timely and timeless.
Written by married couple Matthew and Tori

00:13:50.509 --> 00:13:56.189
Butler-Hart and directed by Matthew, Infinitum
stars Tori as Jane, an amnesiac woman trapped

00:13:56.189 --> 00:14:01.009
in a parallel world — and in a time loop,
to boot. The film features cameos from Sir

00:14:01.009 --> 00:14:07.350
Ian McKellen as Dr. Charles Marland-White
and Conleth Hill as Professor Aaron Östergaard,

00:14:07.350 --> 00:14:11.579
both of whom may have information that could
help Jane regain her memory and return to

00:14:11.579 --> 00:14:12.970
her own world.

00:14:12.970 --> 00:14:18.309
In particular, Infinitum: Subject Unknown
has been praised for Tori Butler-Hart's performance.

00:14:18.309 --> 00:14:23.470
For example, Rachael Harper at SciFiNow writes
that the actress, who's frequently alone on-screen

00:14:23.470 --> 00:14:28.920
in the movie, "shoulders that responsibility
incredibly well," while Keri O'Shea of Warped

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:30.459
Perspective says:

00:14:30.459 --> 00:14:34.600
"[Butler-Hart] pitches it perfectly given
the situations unfolding around her, and she

00:14:34.600 --> 00:14:37.199
does most of it without saying anything."

00:14:37.199 --> 00:14:41.670
Critics have also been impressed by how the
movie tackles its heavy and horrific themes,

00:14:41.670 --> 00:14:44.649
with Sarah Cartland of Caution Spoilers writing
that:

00:14:44.649 --> 00:14:48.999
"[Infinitum is] a chilling story about the
expansion of human life."

00:14:48.999 --> 00:14:55.497
For a sci-fi flick that'll satisfy your cravings
for intensity, go no further than Infinitum: Subject Unknown.

00:14:56.810 --> 00:15:02.110
James Gunn scores again with his take on DC
super-villains in The Suicide Squad. Less

00:15:02.110 --> 00:15:07.670
a sequel to 2016's Suicide Squad and more
a reboot of the title, this film brings back

00:15:07.670 --> 00:15:12.449
Harley Quinn, Amanda Waller, and a handful
of other familiar faces from the original.

00:15:12.449 --> 00:15:17.579
At the same time, however, it brings in a
whole heap of new characters, fronted by Idris

00:15:17.579 --> 00:15:21.279
Elba's Bloodsport and John Cena's Peacemaker.

00:15:21.279 --> 00:15:26.110
Once again, Waller offers a ragtag group of
super-villains the chance to shave time off

00:15:26.110 --> 00:15:31.389
their prison sentences by undertaking a secret
mission. If they go off script, however, she'll

00:15:31.389 --> 00:15:34.069
blow off their heads. Cue the mayhem.

00:15:34.069 --> 00:15:36.379
"We're all gonna die."

00:15:36.379 --> 00:15:38.109
"I hope so."

00:15:38.109 --> 00:15:41.059
The Seattle Times' Soren Andersen writes of
the film:

00:15:41.059 --> 00:15:45.649
"Gunn masterfully mixes humor and bloodshed
and manages to give a surprising number of

00:15:45.649 --> 00:15:50.440
characters room to develop their personas.
And when it comes to staging set pieces, he's

00:15:50.440 --> 00:15:51.990
at his best."

00:15:51.990 --> 00:15:54.079
While Olly Richards of Empire writes:

00:15:54.079 --> 00:15:59.250
"When Gunn took on Guardians of the Galaxy,
he turned nonsense into gold for Marvel. By

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:04.270
giving The Suicide Squad the same sense of
mischief and an equally surreal streak, he's

00:16:04.270 --> 00:16:05.940
done the same for DC."

00:16:06.940 --> 00:16:11.709
Frank Herbert's Dune has long been considered
an unfilmable science fiction novel, but that

00:16:11.709 --> 00:16:16.629
hasn't stopped people from trying. First there
was the 1984 David Lynch film and then the

00:16:16.629 --> 00:16:22.129
2000 miniseries — and now its latest cinematic
incarnation has arrived. And it's pretty darn

00:16:22.129 --> 00:16:27.470
good, too. Director Denis Villeneuve wisely
presents only a portion of Frank Herbert's

00:16:27.470 --> 00:16:33.019
epic original novel in a way that's both satisfying
as a movie in itself and as an introduction

00:16:33.019 --> 00:16:34.449
to the events to come.

00:16:34.449 --> 00:16:40.040
Dune is set in 10191, a year in which House
Atreides is tasked by the Emperor to replace

00:16:40.040 --> 00:16:45.910
the ruthless Harkonnens as overseers of the
planet Arrakis, harvesting its coveted spice

00:16:45.910 --> 00:16:51.339
and keeping an eye on the indigenous Fremen.
The Harkonnens are less than pleased about

00:16:51.339 --> 00:16:54.769
this, of course, and a devastating conflict
quickly erupts.

00:16:54.769 --> 00:17:00.939
Timothée Chalamet stars as protagonist Paul
Atreides, the son of Duke Leto Atreides and

00:17:00.939 --> 00:17:06.569
his consort, Lady Jessica Atreides. He leads
a cast that includes such stars as Zendaya,

00:17:06.569 --> 00:17:11.949
Dave Bautista, and Jason Momoa. Audiences
and critics alike have fallen in love with

00:17:11.949 --> 00:17:16.449
Denis Villeneuve's Dune. For example, Salon's
Melanie McFarland writes:

00:17:16.449 --> 00:17:21.669
"It is a gorgeous sensory immersion that holds
us in its spell for hours, with an ending

00:17:21.669 --> 00:17:23.990
that sparks yearning to see what comes next."

00:17:23.990 --> 00:17:31.100
Meanwhile, The AV Club's Ignatiy Vishnevetsky
notes that, although the story may feel "inconclusive,":

00:17:31.100 --> 00:17:35.940
"The resulting film is a rarity among the
overlong effects-heavy blockbusters of the

00:17:35.940 --> 00:17:39.679
last decade: One actually wishes it didn't
have to end so soon."

00:17:41.260 --> 00:17:47.380
In director Miguel Sapochnik's Finch, which
hit Apple TV+ in November 2021, a solar flare

00:17:47.380 --> 00:17:53.169
has scorched the Earth, turning the planet
into a post-apocalyptic nightmare. Tom Hanks'

00:17:53.169 --> 00:17:58.720
Finch Weinberg, a robotics engineer, has managed
to survive, along with his helper-bot, Dewey,

00:17:58.720 --> 00:18:04.320
and dog, Goodyear. Unfortunately, Finch is
dying. To ensure his pooch's survival after

00:18:04.320 --> 00:18:09.279
his death, Finch creates a new robot to care
for him. This robot chooses "Jeff" for its

00:18:09.279 --> 00:18:14.620
name, but after Finch's work is interrupted,
Jeff is left with the same developmental stage

00:18:14.620 --> 00:18:20.409
as a child. It's then up to Finch to parent
Jeff so that he can grow and care for Goodyear.

00:18:20.409 --> 00:18:25.590
Finch is tailor-made for people who love dogs,
children, robots, and Tom Hanks. And it's

00:18:25.590 --> 00:18:31.779
also a really gorgeous movie. Tomris Laffly
of Variety praises the movie's visuals, adding:

00:18:31.779 --> 00:18:37.000
"Finch offers the right kind of big-budget
cinematic escape to viewers young and old,

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:43.279
one that's as endearingly uplifting and disarming
as a devoted good boy's unconditional company."

00:18:43.279 --> 00:18:47.850
As you might expect, Hanks is phenomenal,
with The Austin Chronicle's Richard Whittaker

00:18:47.850 --> 00:18:48.850
writing:

00:18:48.850 --> 00:18:52.789
"If there is a cinematic embodiment of hope,
it may well be Tom Hanks."

00:18:53.789 --> 00:18:58.679
As you may have heard, many of the reviews
for Marvel Studios' superhero epic, Eternals,

00:18:58.679 --> 00:19:03.250
weren't exactly great. Sometimes, though,
you have to disregard a lousy score on Rotten

00:19:03.250 --> 00:19:07.460
Tomatoes and go with your heart. Or in this
case, the audience score, which stands at

00:19:07.460 --> 00:19:09.419
an impressive 80%.

00:19:09.419 --> 00:19:14.110
Academy Award-winning director Chloé Zhao
grounds this cosmic tale of a race of immortal

00:19:14.110 --> 00:19:19.670
beings in basic human emotions such as love
and sacrifice. The ten Eternals arrive on

00:19:19.670 --> 00:19:24.850
Earth to defeat the Deviants, aliens who are
hell-bent on destruction. Once that's done,

00:19:24.850 --> 00:19:30.059
the Eternals remain to observe and occasionally
assist humanity throughout history, but they're

00:19:30.059 --> 00:19:35.169
instructed by their Celestial leader, Arishem,
not to interfere in human affairs. However,

00:19:35.169 --> 00:19:41.490
thousands of years of observing the worst
of human behavior begins to wear on some Eternals,

00:19:41.490 --> 00:19:47.289
and when Arishem finally reveals the Eternals'
true purpose and nature, all hell breaks loose.

00:19:47.289 --> 00:19:52.669
It's worth bearing in mind that not all critics
slammed this film. Nina Li Coomes of the Chicago

00:19:52.669 --> 00:19:53.710
Reader says:

00:19:53.710 --> 00:19:58.340
"[Eternals is a] genuine, delightful character
development nestled inside of a complex and

00:19:58.340 --> 00:19:59.820
challenging narrative."

00:19:59.820 --> 00:20:03.440
Speaking in defense of the film, Peter Travers
of ABC News writes:

00:20:03.440 --> 00:20:07.549
"What you'll remember is the quiet at the
heart of the storm and the intimate connections

00:20:07.549 --> 00:20:12.179
that Zhao values above spectacle. Different?
You bet. But it's also what makes Eternals

00:20:12.179 --> 00:20:14.970
unique and unforgettable."

00:20:15.970 --> 00:20:19.650
Spider-Man: No Way Home earned a sparkling
slate of reviews and reached dizzying heights

00:20:19.650 --> 00:20:25.120
in box office revenue in record time. In this
installment in the MCU, Peter Parker's identity

00:20:25.120 --> 00:20:30.500
has been revealed to the world, forcing immense
pressure onto Parker and his friends and family.

00:20:30.500 --> 00:20:35.300
With nowhere else to go, Parker asks Doctor
Strange for help — and inadvertently tears

00:20:35.300 --> 00:20:37.253
open the walls of the multiverse.

00:20:37.253 --> 00:20:40.403
"Hello Peter,"

00:20:41.090 --> 00:20:45.800
Spider-Man: No Way Home has received especially
high marks for being a film both diehard fans

00:20:45.800 --> 00:20:50.620
and casual filmgoers can enjoy. Cary Darling
of the Houston Chronicle wrote:

00:20:50.620 --> 00:20:56.409
"[No Way Home is] a deft, funny and surprisingly
emotional superhero adventure that manages

00:20:56.409 --> 00:21:00.870
to provide both fan service for the Marvel
faithful and breezy entertainment for those

00:21:00.870 --> 00:21:03.340
who wouldn't know Peter Parker from Peter
Jackson."

00:21:03.340 --> 00:21:07.070
Empire's Dan Jolin offered similar praise,
and added that:

00:21:07.070 --> 00:21:10.530
"It won't just make you cheer, it'll make
you want to hug your friends, too."

00:21:11.530 --> 00:21:16.720
In the German sci-fi romance I'm Your Man,
Maren Eggert stars as Alma, an archeologist

00:21:16.720 --> 00:21:22.559
who agrees to participate in a unique three-week
study. The product being evaluated is a robot

00:21:22.559 --> 00:21:28.029
named Tom, who has been programmed to be her
perfect match. Depending on Alma's verdict,

00:21:28.029 --> 00:21:32.630
Tom and others like him could be granted human
rights. Can artificially intelligent beings

00:21:32.630 --> 00:21:38.070
fulfill the romantic needs of someone who's
single? Should such robots have freedom? And

00:21:38.070 --> 00:21:40.820
can Alma even begin to answer those questions?

00:21:40.820 --> 00:21:46.270
The deep ambition that fuels I'm Your Man
has been rewarded with widespread acclaim.

00:21:46.270 --> 00:21:48.610
Stephanie Zacharek of Time magazine says:

00:21:48.610 --> 00:21:53.700
"I'm Your Man is funny in such a gentle way
that you may not realize how piercing it is

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:56.370
until after the credits have rolled."

00:21:56.370 --> 00:22:02.320
The A.V. Club's A. A. Dowd also praised the
cast "for keeping the balance of frothy-to-thoughtful"

00:22:02.320 --> 00:22:06.419
right where it should be. This unique film
has more on its mind than whether the girl

00:22:06.419 --> 00:22:09.110
gets the boy, and is all the better for it.

00:22:10.350 --> 00:22:15.850
In The Matrix Resurrections, director Lana
Wachowski returns to the dystopia of the Matrix.

00:22:15.850 --> 00:22:20.740
Keanu Reeves is back as Thomas Anderson, this
time working as a famous game developer whose

00:22:20.740 --> 00:22:26.700
most popular creation is a video game called
The Matrix. But Mr. Anderson keeps flashing

00:22:26.700 --> 00:22:31.751
back to another life. While his therapist
tries to keep him grounded in "reality," it's

00:22:31.751 --> 00:22:37.310
not long before Neo is back in the real world
and eager to search for his true love, Carrie-Anne

00:22:37.310 --> 00:22:43.389
Moss' Trinity. Trinity believes she is Tiffany,
however, a normal mother and wife, and it's

00:22:43.389 --> 00:22:45.900
up to Neo to free her from this new life.

00:22:45.900 --> 00:22:50.450
The Matrix Resurrections could easily have
been a soulless cash-grab, but instead, it's

00:22:50.450 --> 00:22:55.960
a deeply romantic and hopeful film that deconstructs
nostalgia and explores complex ideas such

00:22:55.960 --> 00:23:02.179
as choice and purpose. Vox's Emily VanDerWerff
praised the film for its nuanced portrayal

00:23:02.179 --> 00:23:05.669
of trauma, while Noëlle D. Lilley of The
Chicago Reader wrote:

00:23:05.669 --> 00:23:10.860
"The Matrix Resurrections is a breath of fresh
air in a time when reboots are a dime a dozen."

00:23:12.799 --> 00:23:19.019
Director Sion Sono's gore-soaked sensibilities
are on full display in Prisoners of the Ghostland.

00:23:19.019 --> 00:23:23.860
Nicolas Cage gives a superb performance as
Hero, a criminal who is forced by the Governor

00:23:23.860 --> 00:23:29.750
of Samurai Town to rescue a kidnapped girl
named Bernice from the mysterious Ghostland.

00:23:29.750 --> 00:23:35.270
This unique movie mashes up science fiction,
western grit, dystopian drama, horror, and

00:23:35.270 --> 00:23:39.780
samurai cinema into one completely unforgettable
experience.

00:23:39.780 --> 00:23:44.429
While some critics found this collision of
genre a little much, others praised Prisoners

00:23:44.429 --> 00:23:49.650
of the Ghostland for its sheer audacity. The
Independent's Clarisse Loughrey said:

00:23:49.650 --> 00:23:53.909
"Sono may indulge in madness, but it's not
madness without reason."

00:23:53.909 --> 00:23:56.559
Meagan Navarro of Bloody Disgusting writes:

00:23:56.559 --> 00:24:01.990
"[Prisoners of the Ghostland] is a neon-drenched
spaghetti western full of idiosyncratic humor

00:24:01.990 --> 00:24:07.039
and violent social messaging [...] a triumph
for bizarro cinema."

00:24:07.039 --> 00:24:12.390
The film has also received kudos for its commitment
to an out-there storyline, while Screen Daily's

00:24:12.390 --> 00:24:17.750
Tim Grierson praised Cage's performance as
the "high-spirited" heart of the production.

00:24:17.750 --> 00:24:21.850
Although it's not for the faint-hearted, Prisoners
of the Ghostland is very much a thrill ride

00:24:21.850 --> 00:24:22.860
worth taking.

